          ],
          "content": "6"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.071,
            0.495,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.11,
            0.883,
            0.175
          ],
          "content": "4. If x = y, then $a + x = a + y$ , where x, y and a are any three numbers. This proposition and its converse are routinely used while solving equations."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.178,
            0.806,
            0.201
          ],
          "content": "5. If a and b are perfect squares, then ab is a perfect square."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.209,
            0.627,
            0.231
          ],
          "content": "In Questions 6 and 7, x and y are real numbers."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.233,
            0.4,
            0.256
          ],
          "content": "6. If x = y, then $\\boldsymbol { x } ^ { 2 } = \\boldsymbol { y } ^ { 2 } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.258,
            0.4,
            0.281
          ],
          "content": "7. If $x = y ,$ then $\\boldsymbol { x } ^ { 3 } = \\boldsymbol { y } ^ { 3 } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.29,
            0.554,
            0.312
          ],
          "content": "In Questions 8–12, n is a positive integer."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.32,
            0.792,
            0.342
          ],
          "content": "8. If n is divisible by 24, then it is divisible by both 4 and 6."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.345,
            0.804,
            0.366
          ],
          "content": "9. If n is divisible by 60, then it is divisible by both 5 and 12."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.37,
            0.881,
            0.392
          ],
          "content": "10. If n is the square of a prime number, then it has exactly 3 factors."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.395,
            0.881,
            0.436
          ],
          "content": "11. If n is a product of two unequal prime numbers, then it has exactly 4 divisors."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.439,
            0.881,
            0.48
          ],
          "content": "12. If n and n + 3 have no factors in common, then n is not a multiple of 3."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.484,
            0.881,
            0.523
          ],
          "content": "13. There are no known ‘neat’ expressions that generate only primes! Find counterexamples to the following claims."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.195,
            0.522,
            0.697,
            0.542
          ],
          "content": "(i) All numbers of the form $4 n ^ { 2 } + 1$ are prime."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.188,
            0.541,
            0.734,
            0.561
          ],
          "content": "(ii) All numbers of the form $n^{2}+n+$ 11 are prime."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.182,
            0.561,
            0.686,
            0.582
          ],
          "content": "(iii) All numbers of the form $4^{n} + 3$ are prime."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.591,
            0.736,
            0.611
          ],
          "content": "14. Find counterexamples to the following statements."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.195,
            0.61,
            0.812,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": "(i) If n is a prime number, then $2 ^ { \\mathrm { n } } - 1$ is a prime number."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.189,
            0.63,
            0.815,
            0.65
          ],
          "content": "(ii) If n is an even number, then $2 ^ { \\mathrm { n } } + 1$ is a prime number."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.654,
            0.881,
            0.693
          ],
          "content": "15. Consider the statement: ‘If a number is divisible by 8, then it is divisible by both 2 and 4’."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.198,
            0.693,
            0.473,
            0.713
          ],
          "content": "(i) Justify the statement."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.189,
            0.712,
            0.883,
            0.79
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Recall the divisibility shortcuts that we have studied for different numbers. To check whether a given number is divisible by 8, is it enough to check whether it is divisible by 2 and 4? Why or why not?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.793,
            0.884,
            0.892
          ],
          "content": "16. Recall that a shortcut to check whether a given number is divisible by 3 is to add the digits of the number and check if the sum is a multiple of 3. Express the relationship between ‘a number is divisible by 3’ and ‘sum of the digits is a multiple of $3 ^ { \\circ }$ using ‘If-then’ sentences."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
